      "preview_head": "by Thomas Nowak (EHPA) Heat pump technology has seen tremendous technological advances over the past decade. Capacities, operation efficiencies at low temperatures and the possible temperature level t",
      "preview_tail": "mperature air-source and ground source systems may overcome some limitations related with radiator size or flow temperatures which were typically an obstacle for older buildings when trying adopt such",
      "full_text": "by Thomas Nowak (EHPA) Heat pump technology has seen tremendous technological advances over the past decade. Capacities, operation efficiencies at low temperatures and the possible temperature level to be provided, all have increased. In particular the latter is interesting when it comes to the question whether or not heat pumps can be deployed outside the new build, residential sector. Are heat pumps suitable for renovation, the sleeping giant of energy efficiency in Europe's building stock? The answer is threefold: Any building that is undergoing significant renovation and reaches the energy efficiency of a new building can be heated (and increasingly often cooled) by heat pump technology. Buildings that undergo only moderate renovation (for example replacement of windows, roof insulation or facade refurbishment), but still have limitations in terms of their envelope's thermal performance, may also benefit from the use of hybrid solutions combining a gas boiler with an air-source or ground-source heat pump. Such systems can offer improved efficiency compared to traditional fossil fuel-based heating and provide flexibility for varying building loads. However, buildings that are only subject to minor renovations (e.g., replacing individual windows) might not be ideal candidates due in part because the overall thermal performance of their envelope does not allow them to take full advantage of heat pump technology's potential. Despite this challenge, there remain opportunities even here: modern high-temperature air-source and ground source systems may overcome some limitations related with radiator size or flow temperatures which were typically an obstacle for older buildings when trying adopt such",
